      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(mattermost): truncate inbound preview on code-point boundary",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe Mattermost inbound handler in `extensions/mattermost/src/mattermost/monitor.ts` builds a verbose log preview with `bodyText.slice(0, 200)`. When an emoji or other astral character straddles the 200 UTF-16 code-unit boundary, that raw slice can emit a **lone surrogate** into the preview string. Downstream consumers that expect well-formed UTF-16 can reject or mis-render the preview.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nReplace the raw `.slice(0, 200)` with the shared `truncateUtf16Safe` helper from `openclaw/plugin-sdk/text-utility-runtime`, matching the surrogate-safe preview pattern already used by Slack, Discord, Telegram, IRC, Line, MS Teams, and Feishu channel previews.\n\n## User Impact\n\nCosmetic logging only: when the 200th code unit would split an emoji, the verbose inbound preview now omits the partial emoji instead of logging a broken surrogate. No message delivery or routing behavior changes.\n\n## Evidence\n\n```bash\nnode scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/mattermost/src/mattermost/monitor-inbound-preview.test.ts --run --reporter=verbose\n```\n\n## Real behavior proof\n\n- **Behavior or issue addressed:** Mattermost verbose inbound log previews must not contain lone UTF-16 surrogates when truncation lands inside an emoji surrogate pair.\n- **Real environment tested:** macOS (Darwin), Node.js v22.22.0.\n- **Exact steps or command run after this patch:**\n  1. `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/mattermost/src/mattermost/monitor-inbound-preview.test.ts --run --reporter=verbose`\n- **Evidence after fix:**\n\n```text\n$ node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/mattermost/src/mattermost/monitor-inbound-preview.test.ts --run --reporter=verbose\n ✓ does not split a surrogate pair at the 200-unit preview cap\n ✓ escapes newlines after surrogate-safe truncation\n\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  2 passed (2)\n```\n\n- **Observed result after fix:** For a body with 199 `a` characters followed by 😀, raw `slice(0, 200)` contains a lone high surrogate; the Mattermost preview path using `truncateUtf16Safe` returns 199 well-formed `a` characters with no surrogate halves.\n- **What was not tested:** Live Mattermost websocket ingest against a real server; regression test imports the same `truncateUtf16Safe` helper used by `monitor.ts`.\n\n## Tests and validation\n\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/mattermost/src/mattermost/monitor-inbound-preview.test.ts --run --reporter=verbose`\n- Regression tests in `extensions/mattermost/src/mattermost/monitor-inbound-preview.test.ts`\n\n## Risk checklist\n\n- Did user-visible behavior change? Yes — verbose log preview may omit a trailing emoji when truncation would have split its surrogate pair.\n- Did config, environment, or migration behavior change? No\n- Did security, auth, secrets, network, or tool execution behavior change? No\n- Highest-risk area: verbose logging only\n- Mitigation: matches sibling channel preview truncation behavior; message handling unchanged\n\n## AI-assisted disclosure\n\n- [x] AI-assisted (Cursor agent)\n- [x] Human-run Real behavior proof from local setup\n",
        "branch": "fix/mattermost-monitor-utf16-truncation",
